{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a pragmatic, non-inductive decision rule based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) rather than probability.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude cannot be defined without relying on inductive assumptions about the future resembling the past.","If the act of 'preferring' for action logically entails an expectation of future success equivalent to assigning higher probability."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the rational basis for action. We prefer the theory that has withstood the severest tests not because we believe it is more likely to be true (induction), but because it is the best candidate for approximating truth (verisimilitude) among available options. This is a critical preference, not a probabilistic one.","optional_refs":["9e8a2b515584060643afad08f2e890b2f0afda5c3ca33222164380aabb8aab40","bc2379043ea759a5add3940f7e6d589617b59d462b8d8bd10b8cd5d83c87a2bf"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles in induction, rendering Popper's position internally inconsistent when applied to practical scientific action.","counterconditions":["If a non-inductive basis for future expectation can be rigorously formulated (e.g., via propensity interpretations that avoid uniformity).","If 'preference' can be shown to be purely aesthetic or arbitrary without impacting the claim of rationality."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To prefer Theory A over Theory B for action implies an expectation that A will perform better in the future. Since past performance (corroboration) cannot logically guarantee future performance without the principle of uniformity of nature (induction), any such preference is implicitly inductive. Popper's attempt to separate 'corroboration' from 'probability' fails at the point of decision.","optional_refs":["82d932993cea062a6ca0f4ca09c967de8d45396d0d458593d653fb6cb41f9376","48c4058b65566c1916052587394c85e201b43ffc6160053f0587893fb9cd43a7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible not as an inference to truth or probability, but as a methodological commitment to error elimination, where 'better-corroborated' simply means 'has survived more attempts to falsify it so far'.","counterconditions":["If scientists actually require predictive reliability for action, not just a target for falsification.","If the concept of 'severe test' itself relies on inductive background knowledge."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This approach reframes preference as a procedural rule: always test the bold conjecture that has survived the most severe criticism. It makes no claim about the future success of the theory, only that it is the current best target for further testing. Action based on it is a gamble, not a prediction.","optional_refs":["b4203b4ca348f4d29b4cf1fd763801b99bd42082877d081ff97525ffdc66b021","57caaebff03718fcfae1eb65e146728a7ccfcdbb9a565c5c07eec876a7171e21"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is false; Popper's system allows for a 'rational faith' or 'decisionist' element where the preference is an existential choice unsupported by logic, thus neither inductive nor deductive.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly rejected decisionism in favor of critical rationalism.","If this admission undermines the claim that science is purely rational/critical."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper's philosophy contains a decisionist strand where the choice to accept corroboration as a guide is a non-rational (but not irrational) commitment to the game of science. It admits that logic alone cannot bridge the gap, so the scientist makes a voluntary choice to act *as if* corroboration matters.","optional_refs":["5d848ac46e0509ec2b1adfe65e52e96fdfe97e2315ce500bba4be1bfb57964df","469214066cd0fe367482ab1dbf622f3c2ad7e94d48942d7e905fb1fd4b3ff862"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}